Now we know the problem. 👍

This command:

Get-ChildItem -Force | Where-Object {$_.Name -like "*gitignore*"}

returned nothing.

So there is no .gitignore file in your current project root:

C:\Users\Administrator\OneDrive\Desktop\HRMS-Project-main

You probably edited a .gitignore inside another folder.

Fix it

From your current folder, run:

New-Item -Path .gitignore -ItemType File

Then open it:

notepad .gitignore

Add:

# Environment files
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env

Save and close Notepad.

Now test it

Run:

git check-ignore -v database/.env

You should now get something like:

.gitignore:5:*.env    database/.env

Then:

git status

database/.env should no longer appear under Untracked files. ✅

Then commit the .gitignore
git add .gitignore
git commit -m "Add gitignore for environment files"

Finally verify:

git status

You should ideally see:

On branch attendance-module
nothing to commit, working tree clean

Do not add database/.env with git add. Keep that file only on your local computer.

PS C:\Users\Administrator\OneDrive\Desktop\HRMS-Project-main> notepad .gitignore
PS C:\Users\Administrator\OneDrive\Desktop\HRMS-Project-main> git check-ignore -v database/.env
PS C:\Users\Administrator\OneDrive\Desktop\HRMS-Project-main> 

Okay. Since git check-ignore still gives no output, let's verify whether the new .gitignore actually contains the rule.

1. Run this
Get-Content .gitignore

You should see:

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env
2. If you see those lines, run:
git check-ignore -v --no-index database/.env

Notice the --no-index.

You should get something like:

.gitignore:4:*.env    database/.env
3. Then check:
git status

database/.env should no longer appear under Untracked files.

If Get-Content .gitignore shows nothing or gives an error, then the file wasn't saved correctly.

Run these two commands and send me the output:

Get-Content .gitignore
Get-Item .gitignore | Select-Object FullName,Length

Don't worry about database/.env—do not delete it. We are only making Git ignore it.

Ah, now we found the issue. ✅

Your screenshot shows that .gitignore is inside the frontend folder:

HRMS-Project-main
└── frontend
    ├── .env
    ├── .gitignore   ← this one
    ├── package.json
    └── ...

But your database/.env is outside frontend:

HRMS-Project-main
├── database
│   └── .env
├── frontend
│   └── .gitignore
└── ...

Therefore, frontend/.gitignore cannot ignore database/.env.

Do this

From your current root folder:

C:\Users\Administrator\OneDrive\Desktop\HRMS-Project-main

run:

notepad .gitignore

If Notepad asks to create the file, choose Yes.

Put this in the root .gitignore:

# Environment files
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example


# Dependencies
node_modules/


# Build files
dist/

Save it.

Now test

Run:

git check-ignore -v database/.env

You should now get something similar to:

.gitignore:4:*.env    database/.env

Then:

git status

database/.env should disappear from the Untracked files section. ✅

Important

You can keep:

frontend/.gitignore

You don't need to delete it. The new root .gitignore is what protects files across your entire HRMS project.

After that, run:

git add .gitignore
git commit -m "Add root gitignore for environment files"

Then verify:

git status

And do not run git add database/.env.